{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB01146 - MONDO_6004",
  "verbalization": "Diphenylpyraline is implicated in the pathogenesis of Vasomotor Rhinitis through its association with Urticaria and Allergic Rhinitis, which in turn are linked to Vasomotor Rhinitis. The evidence suggests that Diphenylpyraline may act as a potential repurposed drug for Vasomotor Rhinitis, as it is linked to Urticaria and Pheniramine, both of which are known to affect the nasal mucosa and contribute to vasomotor symptoms. The indirect pathway through Isoflupredone Acetate further supports this, as it is associated with Vasomotor Rhinitis. The scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, though the exact mechanism of action remains unclear. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Diphenylpyraline to modulate inflammatory and vascular responses in the nasal mucosa, though further mechanistic validation is required to confirm its therapeutic potential for Vasomotor Rhinitis.",
